window.docContents[148].resolve({"/#exceptions":{"contents":"The monad transformer version of  is very similar to the monad transformer version of .\nAdding exceptions of type  to some monadic action of type   can be accomplished by adding exceptions to , yielding type :\n\n\n\n provides  and  functions to guide the type checker towards the correct  instances.\nThis trick is also useful for :\n\n\n\nThe  instance for  is also very similar to the instance for .\nThe only difference is that it propagates a specific error value, rather than :\n\n\n\nThe type signatures of  and  contain a subtle detail: they annotate the universe levels of  and  explicitly.\nIf they are not explicitly annotated, then Lean generates a more general type signature in which they have distinct polymorphic universe variables.\nHowever, the definition of  expects them to be in the same universe, because they can both be provided as arguments to .\nThis can lead to a problem in the  instance where the universe level solver fails to find a working solution:\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThis kind of error message is typically caused by underconstrained universe variables.\nDiagnosing it can be tricky, but a good first step is to look for reused universe variables in some definitions that are not reused in others.\n\nUnlike , the  datatype is typically not used as a data structure.\nIt is always used as a control structure with its  instance.\nThis means that it is reasonable to lift  actions into , as well as actions from the underlying monad .\nLifting  actions into  actions is done by wrapping them in 's , because an action that only has exception effects cannot have any effects from the monad :\n\n\n\nBecause actions from  do not have any exceptions in them, their value should be wrapped in .\nThis can be accomplished using the fact that  is a superclass of , so applying a function to the result of any monadic computation can be accomplished using :\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Monad Transformers\u0009A Monad Construction Kit","header":"6.2.2. Exceptions","id":"/#exceptions"}});